      "document_classification": "MIXED",
      "inputText": "I studied creative writing at university alongside English literature. Each week we had a writers’ workshop, sharing work written to a prompt we’d been given the week before.\n\nThe problem was that, because it was mainly a literature course, most tutors were fixated on literary fiction. They always questioned the meanings, symbolism, and point of everything we wrote. God forbid you wrote something without a deeper meaning.\n\nNow, after graduating, I can’t write a damn thing. Any idea brings a voice saying, “Why? What’s the point? What message are you trying to get across?” It stops me stone-dead.\n\nI’ve tried short stories in different genres to get going, but the same roadblock appears every time. I don’t mind writing literary fiction now and again, but it’s hard work, and I just want to enjoy writing the way I used to, without so much meta-thought.\n\nHas anyone else had similar issues, and how did you cope?\n\nEdit: Huge thanks to everyone. Your feedback is helping and making me want to write again. I’d reply to every comment if I could.\n",
